    {"answer": "D", "reason_rejected": "Changing experimental procedures is a methodological adjustment used to fix errors or test new variables. While it might be necessary if the original experiment was flawed, it is not the standard process for validating a sound hypothesis; duplication is the primary test of reliability."},
    {"answer": "B", "reason_rejected": "Revising the hypothesis implies the original was incorrect or incomplete, so it does not lead to the acceptance of the original hypothesis."},
    {"answer": "C", "reason_rejected": "Developing a theory is a later stage in the scientific process that occurs only after a hypothesis has been widely accepted and supported by extensive reproducible evidence."}
  ]
